          "session_id": "full-g-md-008",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines about eight times a month. Is that the level where guidance describes prevention, and what lifestyle changes are described?",
          "answer": "If you have a sudden, severe headache that feels like the worst of your life, or a headache with weakness, confusion, trouble speaking, or vision loss, seek emergency care right away. This safety advice is general and is not drawn from the documents I searched.\n\nYour eight migraine days a month are above the usual threshold for prevention in the National headache society guidance (the migraine acute and preventive treatment document). That guidance offers preventive treatment when attacks are frequent or disabling enough that acute treatment alone is not enough, and it gives four or more migraine days a month with some disability as the usual threshold. Fewer attacks can also qualify if they are severely disabling, if acute treatment does not work or cannot be used, or if the person prefers prevention. Whether this applies to you depends on how much the attacks disrupt your work, sleep and daily life, so a clinician needs to assess that with you. The guidance aims for fewer and milder attacks rather than none, and a 50 percent drop in migraine days is the usual measure of a good response.\n\nOn lifestyle, the consultation search found that the migraine guidance describes sleep regularity as helpful, because sudden changes in sleep timing are the part most often linked to attacks. A second search did not return that passage and found no lifestyle measures for migraine at all, so the material on this point is thin. Neither search found anything on meal timing, hydration, trigger tracking or regular exercise for migraine, and I have not filled those gaps from general knowledge. The sources also do not say how strong the evidence behind the four-day threshold is, or whether other authorities use a different threshold, so I cannot say whether guidance disagrees on this.\n\nWhen you see a clinician, it would help to ask whether your attacks meet the prevention threshold given their impact on your life, which sleep routine changes make sense for you, and whether keeping a diary of attacks, sleep and possible triggers would help. Also ask how often you take rescue medication, because the guideline warns that using it too often can cause headaches of its own.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 17155.999115999977,
